Subject: [PATCH] Wind stress and ice drift checks: [output]
 surface_stress_hours, wind_stress_check.jl, ice_drift_observations.jl
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The Greenland Sea ice, the water under it and the open-water surface ocean are ~1.6× faster than GLORYS in storms and
match it on calm days. Two questions: do we apply more stress than NEMO for the same wind, and is GLORYS (forced by
ERA-Interim, weak in strong winds) the slow one?

- [output] surface_stress_hours: snapshots of u★ of the atmosphere–ocean and atmosphere–sea-ice fluxes, the regridded
  wind and the ice concentration, in arctic_surface_stress.jld2.
- analysis/wind_stress_check.jl: our effective drag coefficient u★²/U² by wind-speed bin, over open water and compact
  ice, against CORE's (Large & Yeager 2009 neutral over water, 1.63e-3 over ice), Greenland Sea and whole domain.
- analysis/ice_drift_observations.jl: ice speed by region, model / GLORYS / NSIDC Polar Pathfinder daily ice motion
  (NSIDC-0116 v4.1, downloaded by hand: Earthdata login).
- config_smoke_test_stress.toml (100 km, 4 days); config_sep2005_30d_nemo_nz100_stress.toml (30 days from 1 Sep 2005,
  as the four-month ne094 run, 3-hourly stress snapshots).

